 ### [MultiDDA](3123-multidda.md)
-MultiDDA is a ray traversal algorithm for sparse voxel grids that uses a two-level Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) to skip empty space efficiently. It builds on [Voxel Ray Marching with Cube-Level DDA](3069-voxel-ray-marching-with-cube-level-dda.md) by structuring the grid into bricks of $8^3$ voxels, allowing the ray to step through the top-level brick grid before entering the inner voxel loop. It differs from its parent by explicitly separating the traversal into two distinct DDA loops: one for the brick map and one for the voxel sub-grid within occupied bricks.
+MultiDDA is an acceleration method for volume rendering that performs incremental Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) traversal over a two-level hierarchical grid to skip empty space more efficiently than a flat grid. It builds on [Voxel Ray Marching with Cube-Level DDA](3069-voxel-ray-marching-with-cube-level-dda.md) by replacing the single-level voxel traversal with a nested approach: a coarse traversal over "bricks" (8³ voxel blocks) followed by a fine traversal over individual voxels only within occupied bricks. It differs from its parent by explicitly decoupling the empty-space skipping logic into a separate coarse grid layer, whereas the parent uses a fixed-size cube-level DDA that may not align with the data structure's logical blocks.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of cubes and voxels traversed along the ray, bounded by a maximum of 49 steps per cube and 49 steps per voxel [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Low, as it operates entirely in the fragment shader with minimal state storage [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Efficient for sparse voxel grids due to empty space skipping at the cube level [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, GPU-friendly, allows runtime voxel changes, supports viewing from inside or outside the volume [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Fixed cube size (16x16x16) may not be optimal for all grid densities; transparency handling relies on screen texture sampling which can have limitations [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Real-time voxel rendering in games or applications where performance is critical and voxel data is sparse [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When adaptive step sizes are required for highly non-uniform voxel densities, or when complex transparency effects beyond simple transmittance are needed [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of cubes and voxels traversed along the ray, bounded by the maximum step counts (49 cubes, 49 voxels per cube).
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Constant per fragment, storing DDA state and a few accumulators.
+*   **Performance:** Designed for GPU fragment shaders, allowing for good performance and runtime changes to voxels at relatively low cost [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, reusable DDA algorithm, supports custom samplers for PBR materials, allows viewing from inside or outside the volume [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Fixed cube size (16x16x16) may not be optimal for all data structures; transparency handling relies on screen texture sampling which can cause limitations with color filtering and shadows [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When rendering voxel volumes on the GPU with a need for simple, reusable ray marching code and support for custom PBR materials.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When adaptive step sizing is required for highly sparse or variable-density volumes, or when advanced shadow casting for transparent voxels is needed.
 
 ## Background
-This method is based on a 2-level DDA algorithm implemented 100% on the GPU as a fragment shader, as described in the Godot Voxel addon documentation [S1, unofficial]. It was developed by Viktor Ferenczi for the Godot Engine [S1, unofficial]. The approach allows for good performance and enables runtime changes to voxels at a relatively low cost [S1, unofficial].
+The method is based on an efficient 2-level DDA algorithm, implemented 100% on the GPU as a fragment shader, as part of the "Voxel rendering addon for Godot 4.3+" [S1, unofficial]. It was developed by Viktor Ferenczi and is available on the Godot Asset Library [S1, unofficial]. The algorithm is designed to be reusable, allowing for custom samplers and material definitions without modifying the core raymarching logic [S1, unofficial].
 
